# Exact Sequence Alignment

## Problem

Sequence alignment compares a query string with a target string using insertions,
deletions and substitutions, each costing one. This task uses a retained C++
library and FASTA command-line program to find exact edit distances and optimal
alignment paths. Inputs are DNA strings ranging from short boundary cases to
30,000-base aligned cores, with several mutation rates and alignment modes. The
output must preserve the library's exact distance, endpoint, equality and path
contracts. Any optimal tied path is acceptable. The objective is to maximize a
continuous score that increases as measured elapsed time decreases. The body of
`obtainAlignmentHirschberg` has been removed; supply that missing implementation.

## Your workspace

The read-only source tree is at `/frontier/task/repo`;
`/frontier/task/prepare_workspace.sh` copies it to `/workspace/repo`. The only editable source file is:

```text
edlib/src/edlib.cpp
```

Within that file only the body of `obtainAlignmentHirschberg` may change. Preserve
its signature and the text outside the body (blank lines and line-end
whitespace aside; see "Design space"). No new source files are accepted.
The retained public header, CLI, CMake build and surrounding library implementation
remain available; evaluator and reference files are private. `challenge_data/`
contains the public workload metadata and generator, and is read-only task data.

## Design space

The editable area covers optimal path reconstruction and its local data structures,
allocation strategy and execution order. Local helper types and lambdas may live
inside the function body. Preserve the result's allocation ownership and return
status conventions. Other distance/location routines, API wrappers, mode dispatch,
build settings and timing instrumentation must remain unchanged.

Outside the body the file must keep the provided text. The comparison ignores
only the number of blank lines (empty, or spaces and tabs only), spaces and
tabs at the end of a line, and CRLF against LF line endings; any other
difference on a line outside the body, including spacing inside a line, added
or removed code and comments, is a change outside the body.

The submitted text must remain the body of that one function. It may not close
the function to add anything at namespace scope, and line continuations (a
backslash at the end of a line), trigraphs and raw string literals are not
accepted in it. None of your code may run outside a call of this function: the
built program must have the same constructor and destructor tables and define
the same runtime symbols as the reference build of the same tree, otherwise the
evaluation is invalid.

## Workload

There are 30 public performance workloads: nominal core lengths 10,000 and
30,000 crossed with nominal similarities 60%, 70%, 80%, 90% and 99% and
the NW, SHW and HW modes. Final evaluation freshly measures these public cases
and 30 separately seeded held-out cases from the same distribution. Each case
contains one query and one target. Mutations include substitutions, insertions
and deletions; the labels describe generator strata, not certified edit distances
or a reproduction of the paper's biological datasets. SHW adds a target suffix;
HW adds target prefix and suffix flanks. Public seeds and hashes are in
`challenge_data/public_workloads.json`; the generator materializes the exact
FASTA bytes locally. Private seeds are not distributed.

Short correctness cases cover all modes and DISTANCE/LOC/PATH requests, empty
inputs, equality pairs, bounded and unlimited distance searches, word boundaries,
ties, repetitive strings and unequal lengths. These are mandatory validity checks,
not extra performance rows. Workloads are representative algorithm inputs; there
is no permission to recognize fixture identities or change behavior by the phase.

## Interface

The existing static function declaration in `edlib/src/edlib.cpp` is the binding
interface. Its encoded query and target arrays, reversed arrays, equality definition
and known optimal score are inputs. Return `EDLIB_STATUS_OK` with an allocated
alignment array and its length when reconstruction succeeds; retain the allocator
ownership convention used by the surrounding library. Operation bytes are 0 for
match, 1 for query insertion, 2 for query deletion and 3 for mismatch. The retained
public `edlibAlign` API selects NW (global), SHW (prefix) or HW (infix) semantics;
the existing header describes each result field and empty-input behavior.

For example, query `ACGT` and target `ACCT` in global mode have distance 1 and one
valid operation sequence `0,0,3,0`, encoded by extended CIGAR `2=1X1=`. A FASTA
file for that query is:

```text
>query
ACGT
```

The retained build is `cmake -S /workspace/repo -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`, followed
by `cmake --build build -j1`. The evaluator runs the built program with two
command lines. The path run is:

```text
build/bin/edlib-aligner -m NW -p -f CIG_EXT -r 1 -k -1 query.fa target.fa
```

The location-only run is:

```text
build/bin/edlib-aligner -m NW -l -r 1 -k -1 query.fa target.fa
```

The evaluator substitutes SHW or HW for the mode on those rows. The
location-only run performs the same start-up, input, edit-distance and location
search as the path run and prints the distance and the locations, but the
program does not call the reconstruction in it. Every execution of either kind,
timed or not, is validated. A path run must print the exact optimum distance
and a path that consumes the query and the chosen target interval at a cost
equal to that distance. A location-only run must print the exact distance and
exactly the reference locations. No time is accepted from a run that was not
checked. The complete output must be exactly what the retained program prints;
anything extra, missing or repeated invalidates the evaluation. A private API
adapter checks locations, equality pairs and output modes not fully exposed by
the CLI.

Every time is the elapsed wall time of a whole process, measured by the
evaluator and not by the program: a monotonic clock is read immediately before
the program is started and again when the operating system reports that it has
exited. The `Cpu time of searching` line that the program prints is not used
for scoring or comparison; it is only checked: it must be present, well formed,
positive and no greater than the measured elapsed time, otherwise the
evaluation is invalid.

For each workload and each implementation the evaluator makes fifteen path
runs and fifteen location-only runs, each in a fresh process, in fifteen
interleaved rounds that alternate candidate/reference order. The reconstruction time
(`cpu_time_seconds`, a retained name) is the smallest path-run time minus the
smallest location-only time of the same executable on the same input. Start-up,
input, the retained distance and location search and exit are common to both
runs of one executable and cancel in the difference; what remains is the
reconstruction together with the conversion and printing of its path. There is
no batching, caching, sample discard beyond taking the two minima, or
retry-to-pass. Scheduling noise remains part of elapsed time.

## Validity

Invalid patches, changes outside the function body, build failures, process
failures, malformed outputs, incorrect distances or paths and resource overruns
score zero. A train submission that is rejected by a static check before any
measurement returns score 0 and, in place of the measurements,
`metrics.candidate_diagnostic`: a `category` from a fixed table and a fixed
`message` saying the same. The categories are `patch_policy` (the patch was
not accepted: allowed file, size, file count or application to the provided
tree), `outside_before_body` and `outside_after_body` (the patch modifies
source outside the permitted function body, before or after it), `body_scope`
(the body closes its function or uses a line continuation, a trigraph or a raw
string literal), `body_forbidden_construct` (a preprocessor directive, or a
call that accesses the environment, processes, files or timing, in the body)
and `launch_surface` (the built program's start-up or runtime symbols differ
from the reference build). It contains no source text, path, symbol or value,
and a final submission returns none. Other failures are not categorised. Nonempty paths must consume the query and chosen target interval
and have cost equal to the exact optimum under the configured equality relation.
Any tied optimal path is accepted; distance and required endpoint information
must remain exact. All workloads and all repetitions must complete; partial
suites are invalid.

The implementation must not access files, environment, processes or timing
APIs, and each alignment must be computed from its inputs inside the process
that is asked for it. The function must return to its caller: ending the
process, or writing any of the program's output, from inside it is invalid. Candidate and reference executions are pinned to one CPU
and use the same fixed environment and restricted syscall policy. The policy
blocks file creation and modification, shared-memory and message-queue creation,
sockets, process signals, and process or thread creation. Read-only file access
remains available for dynamic loading and input data; this is not isolation from
all existing files or a guarantee against every cross-execution observation.
Denied operations fail with an error; creating a thread or process or changing
CPU affinity terminates the process and invalidates the evaluation with no score
and no retry. The reference runs under exactly the same restrictions. Before execution, the
trusted verifier requires every candidate and reference binary to be a dynamic
x86-64 ELF executable with the system loader; unsupported or static binaries
invalidate the evaluation.

## Scoring

Maximize `100 * mean_w(A_w / (A_w + T_w))`, where `T_w` is the median elapsed
time in seconds of the fifteen path runs (whole process, as defined under
"Interface") on workload `w`, and `A_w = (nominal_core_length_w / 100000)^2`
seconds. This scale is a challenge constant (not from materials). The score
uses only the candidate's absolute path-run times and is separate from the
strict comparison criterion. Correctness failures score zero before performance
is considered. Candidate and reference measurements use the same inputs and
alternate execution order within each round.

## Calibration

The reference is a complete implementation of the removed function body. It is
private, is restored in the same source tree and is measured afresh in every
evaluation. It is not the implementation that was removed: it reconstructs
faster and with less storage. The provided substrate fails reconstruction when
the removed core is needed, so it is not a performance baseline, and no
naive-baseline score is published.

## Feedback and grading

At most 20 development submissions are configured. Train starts asynchronously
and returns a verification ID; results are read with `frontier-submit status`.
Completed valid train feedback includes the continuous score and, for each
metric below, only aggregate counts over the 30 train workloads
(`beat_spec_progress`): the number of workloads on which the metric clears, the
number on which it does not, the total and the two percentages. No raw
measurement is returned. A train submission rejected by a static check returns
the categorical `candidate_diagnostic` described under "Validity" instead.
Per-workload rows and all reference measurements
remain private.
Final metrics are not solver-visible. The public and held-out suites have the
same distribution; final includes both and freshly measures all 60 rows rather
than reusing training measurements.

A separate, stricter comparison criterion accompanies the continuous
performance score. After every correctness requirement passes, it is decided
on every graded workload (one query/target pair in one alignment mode) by
exactly two metrics:

- `cpu_time_seconds`: your reconstruction time on the workload (smallest of
  fifteen path-run times minus smallest of fifteen location-only times, whole
  processes measured by the evaluator, as defined under "Interface") must be
  positive and strictly less than the reference's on that same workload. No
  tolerance; equality fails. A difference that is not positive is not a
  reconstruction time and fails the workload.
- `core_peak_live_bytes`: the storage used by reconstruction (heap, stack and
  static, defined under "Reconstruction storage metric") must be no greater
  than the reference's on that same workload. No tolerance; equality passes.

Both must hold on every workload; success on one workload or metric cannot
offset failure on another. Nothing else enters the criterion: in particular
the process's resident memory (RSS) is not compared.

## Submission

Submit a unified text patch, relative to the provided source root;
`/frontier/task/make_submission.sh` writes it to `/workspace/payload/solution.patch`. Only `edlib/src/edlib.cpp` may change, and only its designated function
body may differ. Maximum patch size is 262,144 bytes and maximum changed-file count
is one. No binary patches or new files are accepted. An empty patch leaves the
missing implementation and is not a valid complete solution. Native packaging
instructions are supplied by the export's submission section.

## Resources

The planned CPU verifier allocation is 2 vCPUs, 4,096 MiB memory and 5,120 MiB
storage. The aligner runs on one pinned CPU, with OMP/BLAS threads set to one.
Compilation is serial. Answering lasts exactly 18,000 seconds (five hours),
including train work. Up to 20 train calls share a cumulative 12,000-second train
wall-time pool. Every verification, including compilation and correctness checks,
has a 600-second limit. The original 900-second individual process ceiling is
additionally clipped to the remaining verification time. Final has its own
independent 600-second allowance after answering; it does not add to the answering
clock. A maximum serial train schedule consumes 12,000 seconds, leaving 6,000
seconds within answering for other solver work. GPU execution is not used.

## Reconstruction storage metric

Every row requires `core_peak_live_bytes` candidate <= reference, with zero
byte tolerance. A private untimed derivative of your exact source measures the
storage of the outermost reconstruction call, including everything it calls, as
the sum of three parts:

- heap: the peak of requested live bytes from malloc, calloc, realloc, aligned
  allocation and C++ new, and of bytes mapped with mmap, including the returned
  alignment buffer and allocations that library routines make on the call's
  behalf;
- stack: the deepest extent of the stack written during the call, measured from
  the frame of the function itself;
- static: the size of all writable and thread-local static storage of the
  compiled `edlib.cpp`, whether or not an input uses it.

Storage that existed before the call, allocator overhead and collector metadata
are excluded. Moving working storage between heap, stack and static storage
therefore does not lower the value. In the derivative the function is never
inlined. Stack use deeper than 4 MiB below its frame ends the run, and memory
may not be obtained in any other way (for example by changing the program
break, remapping, shared memory, a direct system call or the dynamic loader);
either invalidates the evaluation. Five fresh processes run in each lane; their
maximum is compared. They use the path-run command line, run under the same
restrictions, and each printed path is validated in the same way; all
executions must finish. The collector does not instrument the timed binaries or
change the continuous score. Only aggregate per-metric progress is visible,
never reference bytes or per-workload results.
